PART 4 — KNOWN LIMITS / NOT DONE (honest list)
================================================================================
- Parallel breaker still writes up to 3 fragments for a repeatedly-failing
  task (file written before validation sees it) — same behavior as the
  sequential lane; bounded, acceptable, but worth knowing.
- Startup replan drops the pending queue: a GDD mtime change (or first boot)
  makes AURA the source of truth and re-plan. Output-scan dedup prevents
  duplicate RE-work, and crash-reset in_progress tasks get re-planned rather
  than resumed. Defensible, but it is not literal resume; unchanged from all
  audited versions.
- max-parallel is effectively 1 for FORGE-heavy cycles (devstral:24b ≈14GB vs
  15GB budget) — physics, not a bug. Light roles pair up genuinely.
- Smoke compile-checks cover .py fragments; .gd/.lua/.cpp etc. are reported
  but not compiled (no toolchains assumed).
- GUI "Parallel: Running" label is still an estimate driven by pending>1, not
  a live readout of the executor.
- gui.py is Windows-oriented by design (os.startfile, cmd /c start) — matches
  the target machine; unchanged.

